##
Naming a file directly.

The folder rule gathers what sits beside a program. When the code you want is somewhere
else, an import names it:

    pc run samples/toolkit/Program.pc

An import names one file, and paths are written relative to the file that wrote them, with
forward slashes on every platform. It brings that file and whatever that file imports in
turn — Tally.pc imports Text.pc, so Text arrives without this program mentioning it.

Reach for an import when you want one file. For a group of related types, reach for a
namespace; for a whole build across folders, reach for a project file.
##

import "shared/Tally.pc";

global model Program

    function Main()
        string[] fruit = {"apples", "pears", "figs"};
        integer[] counts = {7, 3, 12};

        Console.WriteLine(Tally.Bar("fruit", 0));

        for i = 0 until fruit.Count()
            Console.WriteLine(Tally.Bar(fruit[i], counts[i]));
        end for
    end function

end model
